Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The third environment: a development environment for survey programming,
in the three panes the brief draws.

  SURVEY MAP (lib/architect/map.ts, pure, 8 tests). The flow as a tree —
  blocks, groups, branches with their arms and otherwise, loops,
  randomizers, pages, questions — and beneath it the survey's other
  programmable objects: named display rules, calculations, quotas. Every
  row is an engine ObjectKey; every row carries its status (rolled up, so a
  collapsed block still shows the red dot inside it) and its dependency
  counts. Search keeps the path to each match; collapse state is per
  container; ← → walk the tree; a selection made anywhere else expands its
  own path. A question placed on two pages appears at both positions.

  WORKSPACE. Whatever is selected is programmed with the editor the Studio
  already has for it: QuestionEditor for a question, NodeEditor for a flow
  element, DisplayRuleCard / CalculationCard for a rule / calculation
  (extracted from the Logic and Calculations tabs, which render the same
  components now). Nothing selected shows the survey at a glance with
  every object that needs attention, clickable.

  INSPECTOR. For a question, the property panel the Studio has always had,
  with one section above it: DEPENDENCIES — used by / depends on / affects,
  from the dependency index, each entry a link that selects that object.
  For a branch, a rule, a calculation or a quota: identity, its condition
  in English, its issues, its dependencies, its definition.

  FOCUS (§14). A view-level flag on the mode context (so Flow and Grid can
  honour it too), toggled by a button or ⌘⇧F through the command registry:
  everything in the map outside the selection's reach ∪ affects dims;
  ancestors of anything in focus stay legible. "FOCUSING ON Q10 · 22
  related objects, everything else dimmed."

  Panes resize by dragging; widths remembered per browser. The outer
  property panel steps aside while Architect is showing (its inspector is
  inside), and returns when you leave.

Engine: containerList / replaceFlowNode in flowTree.ts, so a node edited in
Architect lands in the same slot the Survey Flow panel would write.

Also: the palette cleared its query on OPEN, which raced the first
keystrokes of anyone typing the instant it appeared; it clears on close.

Tests: lib/architect/map.test.ts (8), scripts/architect-mode-test.mjs (32,
clean console). Mutation-checked: focus without `affects`, inspector
direction flipped, dependency links inert — each caught. Sweep green:
multimodal-foundation (updated for three built modes, pinned to
?mode=studio so a remembered mode cannot leak in), grid-mode (45),
context-right-panel, properties-accordion, canvas (38), blocks,
save-integrity, logic-builder, block-actions, flow-dnd, expression-editor.
Studio lib: 135.

